  "question": {
    "text": "Type of amino acids obtained by hydrolysis of proteins is :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>β</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>δ</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>γ</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Proteins are natural polymers composed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math>-amino acids which are connected by peptide linkages.</p><p>The smallest unit to which the protein can be broken down is called amino acids. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math>-amino acids are the amino acids whose one carbon contains both the amino group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced></math> and the carboxyl group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>COOH</mi></mfenced></math>. This type of carbon is called <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math>-carbon. Thus, the amino acid that contains an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math>-carbon is called an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math>-amino acid.</p><p>Proteins upon acidic hydrolysis produce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math>-amino acids.</p><p>Hence, the answer is option B.</p>"
  }
}